        <div id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Tire Pressure Drops in Winter: About 1 PSI per 10°F
          </h2>

          <p>
            The reason cordless tire inflators sell so well in late November is simple physics. Air contracts as it cools, and a tire is a fixed volume of air. The widely used rule of thumb is that tire pressure falls about 1 PSI for every 10°F drop in air temperature. A car that was set to 35 PSI on a 70°F October afternoon will read roughly 31 or 32 PSI on a 30°F December morning without a single leak. That is enough to trip the low-pressure warning on many cars, and it is the main reason the dashboard light comes on during the first real cold snap of the year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Physics in Plain Terms</h3>

          <p>
            Nothing has escaped from the tire; the molecules inside simply move less and push less on the walls. That is why the pressure partly comes back as the tire warms up from driving or as the afternoon warms up. It is also why adding air in a heated garage and then parking outside overnight leaves you low by morning. A 40°F swing between the garage and the driveway is a 4 PSI swing in the tire.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Warning Light Comes On at Dawn</h3>

          <p>
            Tire pressure monitoring systems typically warn when a tire falls well below the placard pressure. The coldest hour of the day is just before sunrise, so the light usually appears on the morning commute and sometimes goes off again by lunch. Treat it as real: an underinflated tire flexes more, runs hotter, wears its shoulders faster, grips worse in slush and costs fuel. Topping off to the placard number is the fix, and a cordless inflator lets you do it in the driveway in five minutes instead of queueing at a gas-station air machine with frozen fingers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Cold, Set to the Door Jamb</h3>

          <p>
            Always check pressure before driving, when the tires are at outdoor temperature, and set them to the number on the sticker inside the driver's door jamb, not the maximum printed on the sidewall. Because temperature keeps falling through winter, plan to check once every two weeks from November to February, and after any big overnight drop. That routine is exactly what a cordless inflator with auto shut-off is built for.
          </p>
        </div>

