        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cordless tire inflator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people with Milwaukee M12 batteries, the Milwaukee M12 Compact Inflator 2475-20 is the best cordless tire inflator: it publishes real airflow, tops off a car tire in under a minute and shuts off automatically. If you own no tool batteries, the Fanttik X8 APEX is the best self-contained option, rated at plus or minus 1 PSI with up to 40 minutes of run time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does tire pressure drop in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tire pressure drops about 1 PSI for every 10°F fall in air temperature. A tire set to 35 PSI at 70°F will read around 31 PSI at 30°F with no leak at all. That drop is why the low-pressure warning light often comes on during the first cold snap and why tires need topping off more often through winter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless tire inflators worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for most drivers. A cordless inflator lets you top off all four tires in the driveway in a few minutes, at the right temperature, without hunting for a working gas-station air machine. Models with auto shut-off stop at your target pressure, and tool-battery versions cost little if you already own the batteries from a drill or impact driver.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a cordless tire inflator fill a completely flat tire?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, but slowly. These inflators are designed for top-offs, adding a few PSI to a tire holding most of its air. Filling from flat takes much longer, uses most of a battery and can push the pump past its duty cycle, so pause to let it cool. It is a way to reach a tire shop, not a substitute for a compressor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I inflate my tires more in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Inflate to the pressure on the driver's door-jamb placard, measured when the tires are cold. Because pressure falls as temperatures drop, you will add air more often in winter, not to a higher number. If you fill tires in a warm garage before parking outside, add a few PSI to compensate for the colder overnight temperature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do cordless tire inflators work in freezing temperatures?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do, but battery capacity drops in deep cold and most lithium chargers will not charge below freezing. Store tool packs indoors and carry them to the car when needed. Self-contained units kept in the car should come inside to charge monthly. A model with a 12V car cord, such as the DeWalt or AstroAI, works even when the battery is cold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How accurate are cordless tire inflator gauges?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most are close enough for routine top-offs. Milwaukee rates its M12 inflator at plus or minus 3%, about 1 PSI at car pressure, and Fanttik rates the X8 APEX at plus or minus 1 PSI. Check one tire against a quality hand gauge each season, and if the inflator reads high or low, adjust your target setting to compensate.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
